.TH umount "" "Administration"
.SH NAME
umount \- unmount a file system
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fB/etc/umount \fIspecial\fR
.SH DESCRIPTION
.HS
umount -- unmount file system
Usage:	/etc/umount special
The block special file 'special' previously mounted by '/etc/mount'
on 'directory' is unmounted and the previous contents of 'directory'
are once again accessible.
A file system can only be unmounted if all its files are closed.
.HE
.B umount
unmounts the file system held on block-special device
.IR special ,
which was previously grafted onto the directory tree by
.BR /etc/mount .
The previous contents of the directory on which it was mounted become
accessible again.
.PP
.B umount
first calls the
.BR umount ()
system call to detach
.I special
from the directory tree; a file system can only be unmounted if all
of its files are closed.
Once the system call succeeds,
.B umount
finds and clears the entry for
.I special
(matched by its final pathname component) in
.B /etc/mnttab
and
.BR /etc/mtab .
.SH FILES
.DS
.ta 16n
\fB/etc/mtab\fR	table of mounted file systems
\fB/etc/mnttab\fR	table of mount options
.DE
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR mount ,
.B umount()
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
.B umount
complains and exits if the
.BR umount ()
system call fails, for example because
.I special
is not currently mounted or one or more of its files is still open.
.SH NOTES
Unmounting requires permission to call
.BR umount (),
which ordinarily only the superuser has.
